{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And he is found in his hand</b> — that witnesses saw him steal him and sell him, and he was found in his hand before the sale. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 21:16). But I do not understand this: if its purpose is to teach that witnesses saw him in his hand before the sale — would it even occur to you that he would be executed for this until witnesses see him steal and sell? It would have been sufficient for Scripture to say <b>And he who steals a man and sells him</b>, and the fact that he is found in his hand is not a complete proof. Rather, this verse comes to teach what we learned in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 85b): one who steals a person is not liable until he brings him into his domain. And the baraita states: \"He stole him but did not sell him\" — and \"he sold him but he is still in his domain\" — he is exempt. The point is to teach that his law is like the law of other thieves regarding property: if he slaughtered or sold [the stolen animal] while it was still in the owners' domain, he is exempt, but if he lifted it up or removed it from the owners' domain, he is liable (Bava Kamma 79a). So too here, this thief must bring the stolen person into his domain; and likewise, if he lifted the youth onto his shoulder and sold him to another, he is liable, for Scripture calls him <b>and he is found in his hand</b> — and his courtyard should not acquire for him more than his hand does.\\n\\nAnd likewise what was stated, \"he sold him but he is still in his domain\" — that the purchaser did not remove him from his domain at all, and even though he gave money for him, since he did not take him out from there, he [the kidnapper] is exempt. And I do not know whether to say that a formal act of acquisition is required, according to the rules of acquisitions, that he must pull him from the seller's domain into the alleyway, or that he must lift him up; or whether this is a decree of Scripture concerning a human being, to say that even if the sale was finalized between them and he acquired through lifting or through pulling in a courtyard belonging to both of them, he would still be exempt until [the victim] leaves the seller's domain and enters the purchaser's domain. And so it appears [to be the correct interpretation].\\n\\nAnd Rashi explained there in his commentary on the Gemara (Sanhedrin 85b): \"he sold him but he is still in his domain\" — meaning the domain of the stolen person himself, and he [the kidnapper] is exempt, because there is no theft at all. But if so, nothing new is taught here beyond what is already the law for monetary thieves.\\n\\nIn any event, the reading of the verse is: \"And he who steals a man, and he is found in his hand, and sells him, shall surely be put to death.\" And it is possible that it was still in its proper sequence, and \"found in the hand of the purchaser\" — that if he stole the person and brought him to his house, and the purchaser came there and he sold him to him, and the purchaser did not take him out from there, he [the kidnapper] is not liable, because the sale was not finalized between them — or even when the sale was finalized, he would be exempt, as I have written."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase 'הביא שם הלוקח' in the third paragraph is translated as 'the purchaser came there,' reading שָׁם as 'there' (to the kidnapper's house). An alternative reading would take שם as a transitive verb ('named'), but the context of physical domain and transfer supports the locative sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:22.023449497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}